  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Viktor tells the kitchen staff to leave through the back and front doors. But the back door is blocked. An immigration major introduces himself to Viktor. Let us listen to their conversation in Russian first.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Попробуй новый вариант блюда.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Шеф, выход через зал закрыт.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Мы выходим по одному человеку.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Половина идёт через зал.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Что вы за идиоты?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Быстро ко мне в кабинет.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "dima",
      "t": "Добрый день, Виктор Петрович.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "dima",
      "t": "Я майор миграционной службы.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "dima",
      "t": "Мы можем пройти в кабинет?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "К сожалению, я не могу.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Мне нужно готовить для гостей.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "dima",
      "t": "Хорошо, я подожду вас здесь.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let us examine the conversation line by line.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Попробуй новый вариант блюда.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Try the new version of the dish.' Max uses the imperative command form of the verb meaning 'to try'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Шеф, выход через зал закрыт.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Chef, the exit through the hall is closed.' Notice how 'chef' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'is closed'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Мы выходим по одному человеку.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'We exit one person at a time.' Chef Viktor combines 'we' with 'person' in a natural Russian turn of phrase.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Половина идёт через зал.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Half goes through the hall.' Chef Viktor links 'half' directly with 'goes' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Что вы за идиоты?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What kind of idiots are you?' Notice how 'what' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'idiot'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Быстро ко мне в кабинет.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Quickly to my office.' Russian omits the present-tense verb 'to be', connecting 'quickly' directly with 'office'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "dima",
      "t": "Добрый день, Виктор Петрович.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Good afternoon, Viktor Petrovich.' Notice how 'good' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'Petrovich'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "dima",
      "t": "Я майор миграционной службы.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I am a major of the migration service.' The character links 'I' directly with 'major' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "dima",
      "t": "Мы можем пройти в кабинет?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Can we go to the office?' The character combines 'we' with 'office' in a natural Russian turn of phrase.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "К сожалению, я не могу.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Unfortunately, I cannot.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'regret' with 'can'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Мне нужно готовить для гостей.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I need to cook for the guests.' Russian builds this impersonally with the dative ('to me' or 'to you') and 'guests'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "dima",
      "t": "Хорошо, я подожду вас здесь.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Alright, I will wait for you here.' Notice how 'good' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'here'. Now let us listen to the conversation once again.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
